BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d03-fila-lab-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Open exercise: predict backlog before running
DESCRIPTION:Developer community\n\nOpen exercise: predict backlog before ru
 nning\n\nSharing an educational queue demo for technical discussion. Chall
 enge: set 20 arrivals/s\, two workers and 0% failures. Before advancing te
 n steps\, write down how much work you expect in the queue. Then stop arri
 vals and estimate drain time.\n\nThe model attempts one message per worker
  per second\; retries stop after three attempts. It has no backoff\, persi
 stence or partitions. The goal is to discuss capacity and recovery\, witho
 ut estimating real broker performance.\n\nIf you try it\, which assumption
  would you change first: capacity\, burst duration or rejection policy? Sh
 are your reasoning using synthetic data.\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recurs
 os/demos/queue?utm_source=community&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=bibliot
 eca-2026-10&utm_content=d03-fila-lab-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/demos/queue?utm_source=community&utm_m
 edium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d03-fila-lab-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d04-backlog-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261023
SUMMARY:A queue buys time\; capacity pays the bill
DESCRIPTION:LinkedIn\n\nA queue buys time\; capacity pays the bill\n\nIn th
 is model\, 20 arrivals per second and two workers leave 18 messages per se
 cond outstanding. After ten steps\, 180 messages are pending.\n\nThe queue
  absorbs the burst without removing the work. With arrivals stopped\, two 
 workers need another 90 steps to drain it. If 20 arrivals/s continue\, bac
 klog keeps growing.\n\nThe experiment is ready for you to change the varia
 bles. Before choosing a queue\, ask: how much delay can the product tolera
 te\, and what is the capacity recovery plan?\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/re
 cursos/laboratorio/sobrecarga?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=social&utm_ca
 mpaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d04-backlog-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/laboratorio/sobrecarga?utm_source=link
 edin&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d04-bac
 klog-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d05-pitch-worker-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261023
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261024
SUMMARY:Resource suggestion: a TypeScript worker for exploring retries
DESCRIPTION:Newsletter pitch\n\nSubject: Resource suggestion: a TypeScript 
 worker for exploring retries\n\nHello\, editorial team.\n\nI would like to
  suggest a free resource for an issue about backend engineering or event-d
 riven systems: a runnable TypeScript starter with tests\, in-memory dedupl
 ication\, bounded retries and a dead-letter example.\n\nSuggested blurb: 
 “Run a small worker\, trigger a failure and follow its retry limit. A st
 arting point for discussing idempotency before adopting a broker.”\n\nRe
 quires Node.js 22 or newer and uses the MIT license. The README explains e
 xecution\; deduplication is process-local and this is not a production-rea
 dy solution. If it fits your selection\, the link below provides the downl
 oad and context.\n\nThank you for considering it\,\nYago Lagrotti Bracco\n
 \nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/templates/worker?utm_source=newsletter&
 utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d05-pitch-wor
 ker-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/templates/worker?utm_source=newsletter
 &utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d05-pitch-wo
 rker-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
