TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d07-guia-filas-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Does the response need the result now?
DESCRIPTION:LinkedIn\n\nDoes the response need the result now?\n\nThat ques
 tion comes before choosing a broker. If the response needs the result imme
 diately\, and traffic and dependencies fit the latency budget\, a direct c
 all may be enough.\n\nA queue makes sense when acceptance and completion c
 an be separate\, bursts exceed sustained capacity and work can be replayed
  safely. That adds decisions about retention\, ordering\, idempotency and 
 failure handling.\n\nI wrote a short guide with criteria and limitations. 
 Use it to review a choice in your context: which requirement actually need
 s asynchronous processing?\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/guias/filas
 ?utm_source=linkedin&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm
 _content=d07-guia-filas-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/guias/filas?utm_source=linkedin&utm_me
 dium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d07-guia-filas-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d08-env-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261027
SUMMARY:A variable name is also a contract
DESCRIPTION:LinkedIn\n\nA variable name is also a contract\n\nA deployment 
 can fail because a required key never reached the environment. To review t
 hat contract\, I built a tool comparing variable names from an .env.exampl
 e reference against another configuration.\n\nThe report highlights missin
 g keys\, extras\, duplicates and invalid lines without reproducing values.
  Processing happens in the browser. Use synthetic values for demonstration
 s: the checker is not a complete dotenv parser and cannot confirm a secret
  is correct.\n\nWhich configuration check would you like to make before th
 e next deployment?\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/ferramentas/env?utm
 _source=linkedin&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_con
 tent=d08-env-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/ferramentas/env?utm_source=linkedin&ut
 m_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d08-env-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d09-idempotencia-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261027
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261028
SUMMARY:What if the message arrives twice?
DESCRIPTION:WhatsApp\n\nWhat if the message arrives twice?\n\nRetries raise
  a question: does repeating this operation repeat its effect? This short e
 xplanation of idempotency separates message identity from the business eff
 ect. Bring a synthetic payment or order example to your team discussion.\n
 \nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/glossario/idempotencia?utm_source=whats
 app&utm_medium=messaging&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d09-i
 dempotencia-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/glossario/idempotencia?utm_source=what
 sapp&utm_medium=messaging&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d09-
 idempotencia-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d10-worker-lab-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261029
SUMMARY:Small lab: test failures before the broker
DESCRIPTION:Developer community\n\nSmall lab: test failures before the brok
 er\n\nHere is an exercise using the library's TypeScript starter. Download
  it and run npm install\, npm test and npm start on Node.js 22+. Read the 
 deduplication test and retry limit before changing code.\n\nNow describe t
 wo scenarios: a repeated message within one process and a repeat after res
 tarting the process. An in-memory Set does not solve the second case. Whic
 h storage and transaction boundary would you use to connect the business e
 ffect to its idempotency key?\n\nThe starter uses the MIT license and is e
 ducational. If you share a solution\, include a test and explain the failu
 re assumption\; use no real customer data.\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/en/recu
 rsos/templates/worker?utm_source=community&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=
 biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d10-worker-lab-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/templates/worker?utm_source=community&
 utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d10-worker-l
 ab-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d11-clone-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261029
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261030
SUMMARY:A benchmark needs context to be useful
DESCRIPTION:LinkedIn\n\nA benchmark needs context to be useful\n\nComparing
  JSON.parse/stringify and structuredClone by the lowest time leaves import
 ant questions unanswered. Which types must survive? How large was the data
 ? Was the runtime the same?\n\nI published an experiment with synthetic JS
 ON data\, warm-up\, alternating method order and downloadable raw samples.
  The dataset does not test Date\, Map\, cycles or transferables. A local m
 easurement does not make one strategy a universal winner.\n\nRead the meth
 od\, run the script in your environment and compare results by size. Which
  use case would change your choice even at a higher execution time?\n\nhtt
 ps://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/benchmarks/json-clone?utm_source=linkedin&ut
 m_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d11-clone-en
URL:https://lagrotti.dev/en/recursos/benchmarks/json-clone?utm_source=linke
 din&utm_medium=social&utm_campaign=biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d11-clon
 e-en
STATUS:TENTATIVE
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d12-pitch-filas-en-biblioteca-2026-10@lagrotti.dev
DTSTAMP:20261017T000000Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261030
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261031
SUMMARY:For your resource list: a queue you can overload
DESCRIPTION:Newsletter pitch\n\nSubject: For your resource list: a queue yo
 u can overload\n\nHello\, editorial team.\n\nI would like to suggest an in
 teractive demo for an issue on reliability: readers adjust arrivals\, work
 ers and failures to observe backlog\, retries and dead letters. No account
  is required.\n\nSuggested blurb: “Before adding a queue\, predict backl
 og from a ten-second burst and test your estimate in the browser.”\n\nTh
 e model is educational\, with no production data\, backoff or persistence.
  The page states those limits and lets readers repeat the exercise. It may
  be a practical companion to a capacity discussion. Here is the link for y
 our review.\n\nThank you\,\nYago Lagrotti Bracco\n\nhttps://lagrotti.dev/e
 n/recursos/demos/queue?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign
 =biblioteca-2026-10&utm_content=d12-pitch-filas-en
